/P 76 0 R
/E (Gender equality is the equal enjoyment of rights, responsibilities, opportunities, and protections by individuals of all genders. It does not imply identical treatment but rather the removal of discriminatory barriers that prevent equal participation in political, economic, and social life. )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
144 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 76 0 R
/E (Gender-based rights refer to the rights and freedoms guaranteed to all individuals irrespective of their sex or gender. These encompass equal access to education, employment, healthcare, political participation, property ownership, freedom of movement, freedom from violence, and equal )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
145 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 76 0 R
/E (protection under the law. International legal instruments recognise these rights as universal and indivisible, placing obligations upon States to eliminate discrimination and ensure substantive equality. )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
146 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 76 0 R
/E (The Vienna Declaration and Programme of Action \(1993\) reaffirmed that while national and regional particularities should be considered, it remains the duty of States to promote and protect all human rights and fundamental freedoms irrespective of their political, economic, cultural, or religious systems. )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
147 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 76 0 R
/E (A de facto authority refers to an entity that exercises effective control over the territory and population of a State without necessarily being formally recognised as its legitimate government by the international community. )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
148 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 76 0 R
/E (Since August 2021, the Taliban have exercised de facto authority over Afghanistan. However, the question of diplomatic recognition remains distinct from their obligations under international human rights law. )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
